# Creation Frontend - Cursor Rules

## Project Overview
This is the React/TypeScript frontend application for Grapery. It's a modern SPA built with React 18, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, and Vite.

## Technology Stack
- **Framework**: React 18
- **Language**: TypeScript
- **Styling**: Tailwind CSS
- **Build Tool**: Vite
- **State Management**: React Hooks (useState, useContext)
- **Routing**: React Router (implied)

## Code Style & Conventions

### General Rules
- Use TypeScript for all new files
- Follow React best practices and hooks rules
- Use functional components only (no class components)
- Keep components small and focused
- Extract reusable logic into custom hooks
- Use meaningful component and variable names

### File Structure
- `src/components/` - Reusable UI components
- `src/pages/` - Page-level components
- `src/lib/` - Utilities and API clients
- `src/styles/` - Global styles
- `src/guidelines/` - Design guidelines

### Component Patterns

#### Functional Components
```tsx
interface Props {
  title: string;
  onAction: () => void;
}

export const MyComponent: React.FC<Props> = ({ title, onAction }) => {
  const [state, setState] = useState<string>('');
  
  return (
    <div className="container">
      <h1>{title}</h1>
      <button onClick={onAction}>Action</button>
    </div>
  );
};
```

### TypeScript
- Always define types/interfaces for props and data
- Use strict mode
- Avoid `any` type, use `unknown` if type is truly unknown
- Use type inference when types are obvious
- Export types alongside components

### Styling
- Use Tailwind CSS utility classes
- Prefer composition over custom CSS
- Use responsive design utilities (`sm:`, `md:`, `lg:`)
- Follow mobile-first approach
- Keep custom CSS minimal

### State Management
- Use `useState` for local component state
- Use `useContext` for shared state
- Extract complex state logic into custom hooks
- Avoid prop drilling - use context when needed

### API Integration
- Use the API client from `src/lib/api.ts`
- Handle loading and error states
- Use async/await for async operations
- Implement proper error handling

### Performance
- Use `React.memo` for expensive components
- Lazy load routes with `React.lazy()`
- Optimize images and assets
- Avoid unnecessary re-renders

### Accessibility
- Use semantic HTML elements
- Add ARIA labels where needed
- Ensure keyboard navigation works
- Maintain proper color contrast
- Test with screen readers

### Testing
- Write unit tests for utilities
- Test component rendering
- Test user interactions
- Use React Testing Library

## Common Patterns

### Custom Hooks
```tsx
export const useStory = (id: string) => {
  const [story, setStory] = useState<Story | null>(null);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);
  const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);
  
  useEffect(() => {
    fetchStory(id)
      .then(setStory)
      .catch(setError)
      .finally(() => setLoading(false));
  }, [id]);
  
  return { story, loading, error };
};
```

### Error Boundaries
- Implement error boundaries for error handling
- Provide fallback UI for errors
- Log errors for debugging

## When Adding New Features
1. Create TypeScript interfaces/types
2. Build reusable components in `components/`
3. Create page components in `pages/`
4. Add API methods in `lib/api.ts`
5. Implement routing if needed
6. Add responsive styles with Tailwind
7. Test on multiple screen sizes
8. Ensure accessibility

## Dependencies
- Use npm for package management
- Pin dependency versions in `package.json`
- Regularly update dependencies
- Review and audit dependencies for security

## Design Guidelines
- Follow the design system in `src/guidelines/Guidelines.md`
- Use consistent spacing and typography
- Maintain visual hierarchy
- Ensure responsive design works on all devices

## Performance Best Practices
- Code split routes
- Lazy load heavy components
- Optimize bundle size
- Use image optimization
- Minimize re-renders

